{% extends "pages/_docs_guide.jinja" %}
{% import "components.jinja" as ui %}
{% import "_api.jinja" as api %}
{% from "data/docs.jinja" import docs_tested %}
{% set guide_slug = 'input-and-deadlines' %}
{% block guide_body %}
<section aria-labelledby="input">
  <h2 id="input">Input, environment, and directory</h2>
  <p>
    {{ api.ref('StdinInput') }}<code>(text=...)</code> encodes text with the
    context's encoding; <code>StdinInput(data=...)</code> passes bytes. Give
    one, not both, and for input too large to hold in memory
    <a href="#stream">stream it instead</a>. {{ api.ref('ExecutionContext') }}<code>.env</code>
    overlays the live parent environment by default, so an empty mapping
    still inherits everything; <code>cwd</code> sets the child's working
    directory. Everything about <em>how</em> a command runs goes to
    <code>run_sync()</code> or <code>run()</code>, never to the builder,
    which rejects <code>cwd=</code>, <code>env=</code>,
    <code>timeout=</code>, and the other execution-option names with a
    <code>TypeError</code> rather than turning them into child flags.
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('input_and_context.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys

from cuprum import Program, ExecutionContext, ProgramCatalogue, StdinInput, sh

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="input")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)
command = python(
    "-c", "import os,sys; print(os.getenv('CUPRUM_EXAMPLE'), sys.stdin.read())"
)
result = command.run_sync(
    context=ExecutionContext(env={"CUPRUM_EXAMPLE": "ready"}),
    stdin=StdinInput(text="hello"),
    timeout=5,
)
assert result.stdout == "ready hello\n"
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="stream">
  <h2 id="stream">Stream input from a producer</h2>
  <p>
    {{ api.ref('StdinInput') }} holds the whole payload in memory. For input
    that is large, or that is produced as the run goes, pass
    <code>stdin=</code>{{ api.ref('StdinStream') }}<code>(chunks=...)</code>
    with an async iterable instead:
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('streaming_stdin.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys
from collections.abc import AsyncIterator

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, StdinStream, sh

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="stream-input")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)


async def rows() -> AsyncIterator[str]:
    for number in range(1, 1001):
        yield f"row {number}\n"


count_lines = python("-c", "import sys; print(sum(1 for _ in sys.stdin))")
result = count_lines.run_sync(stdin=StdinStream(chunks=rows()))
assert result.stdout == "1000\n"
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  <p>
    Cuprum pulls one chunk, writes it, and waits for the write to drain
    before pulling the next, so a slow reader slows the producer down rather
    than letting input pile up in memory. A drained write is not proof that
    the child has read the chunk: some bytes can still sit in the transport
    buffer or the OS pipe. Each chunk is held in full while it is written, so
    yield chunks of a bounded size when peak memory matters.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Chunk types.</strong> A <code>str</code> chunk is encoded with the context's <code>encoding</code> and <code>errors</code>, and a character split across two chunks is still encoded correctly. A <code>bytes</code> chunk is written as it is.</li>
    <li><strong>Producer failure.</strong> If the producer raises, or yields something that is neither <code>str</code> nor <code>bytes</code>, Cuprum terminates the child and raises {{ api.ref('StdinSourceError') }}, with the original exception as <code>__cause__</code>. Cancellation propagates unchanged.</li>
    <li><strong>A child that stops reading.</strong> Closing stdin early, as <code>head</code> does, is not an error. The run continues to the child's exit code, and the cut-short write is reported as a <code>stdin_error</code> event.</li>
    <li><strong>Clean-up.</strong> On every exit path, Cuprum closes the child's stdin and then calls the producer's <code>aclose()</code>, when it has one. Both waits are bounded: a producer whose <code>aclose()</code> takes longer is cancelled, and one that ignores the cancellation may still be cleaning up after the run returns. A generator needs no closing by the caller.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Where the child's output should go to a file rather than the parent, see
    <a href="/cuprum/docs/guides/output/#redirect">Send a stream to a file or the parent</a>.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="environment">
  <h2 id="environment">Compose the environment</h2>
  <p>
    {{ api.ref('cuprum.context', 'EnvMode') }}<code>.OVERLAY</code> is the
    default policy: values resolve against the live <code>os.environ</code>
    when each child is spawned, not against a snapshot, so a variable set
    after a scope starts is still seen. <code>OVERLAY</code> and
    <code>INHERIT</code> compose: each keeps the mode an outer scope chose
    and layers its values over the inherited overlay. <code>REPLACE</code>
    alone draws a boundary: it starts from an empty environment, applies
    only its mapping, and discards every outer overlay. The
    <code>UNSET</code> value removes a variable from a composed child
    environment, which an overlay could not do before.
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('env_modes.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import os
import sys

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, sh
from cuprum.context import EnvMode, UNSET, env

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="env-modes")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)
show = "-c", "import os; print(os.getenv('CUPRUM_DEMO'), os.getenv('CUPRUM_KEEP'))"

os.environ["CUPRUM_KEEP"] = "kept"
with env({"CUPRUM_DEMO": "overlaid"}, CUPRUM_DEMO="won"):
    assert python(*show).run_sync().stdout == "won kept\n"
with env({"CUPRUM_DEMO": UNSET, "CUPRUM_KEEP": UNSET}):
    assert python(*show).run_sync().stdout == "None None\n"
with env({"CUPRUM_DEMO": "only"}, mode=EnvMode.REPLACE):
    assert python(*show).run_sync().stdout == "only None\n"
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  <p>
    Precedence, lowest first: the process's <code>os.environ</code>, read at
    spawn time; ambient scoped overlays, inner values winning, with a nested
    <code>REPLACE</code> discarding everything outside it; then the per-call
    <code>ExecutionContext.env</code> mapping, whose values win overall and
    whose <code>env_mode</code> can also be <code>REPLACE</code>.
    <code>os.environ</code> itself is never mutated. On POSIX the child's
    <code>PATH</code> is what resolves a bare program name, so a replacement
    policy that omits it can make the name fail to resolve; prefer an
    absolute program path, or an
    <a href="/cuprum/docs/guides/policy-and-hooks/#bind">executable binding</a>,
    with a deliberately minimal environment. The effective mode reaches every
    {{ api.ref('ExecEvent') }} and labels the execution and failure
    counters, so a non-zero exit under a replacement policy is
    distinguishable from an ordinary one.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="deadline">
  <h2 id="deadline">Set a deadline</h2>
  <p>
    Pass <code>timeout</code> to <code>run()</code> or
    <code>run_sync()</code> and catch {{ api.ref('TimeoutExpired') }}, which
    subclasses the built-in <code>TimeoutError</code>. On expiry Cuprum asks
    the child to terminate, waits <code>cancel_grace</code> seconds, then
    kills it. Output captured before the deadline stays on the exception, as
    empty strings rather than <code>None</code> when capture is on.
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('slow_command.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys

from cuprum import ExecutionContext, Program, ProgramCatalogue, TimeoutExpired, sh

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="slow")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)
slow = python("-c", "import time; time.sleep(30)")

try:
    slow.run_sync(timeout=0.5, context=ExecutionContext(cancel_grace=0.2))
except TimeoutExpired as exc:
    assert exc.timeout == 0.5
    assert exc.output == "" and exc.stderr == ""
else:
    raise AssertionError("the command should have timed out")
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  <p>
    Cancelling an async run starts the same teardown. Catch the timeout
    separately from a non-zero exit: one means the deadline passed, the
    other that the child finished and failed.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="where">
  <h2 id="where">Where a deadline comes from</h2>
  <p>Cuprum uses the first of these that is set:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The <code>timeout</code> argument to <code>run()</code> or <code>run_sync()</code>.</li>
    <li>{{ api.ref('ExecutionContext') }}<code>.timeout</code>.</li>
    <li>A {{ api.ref('ScopeConfig') }}<code>(timeout=...)</code> default from the enclosing <code>scoped()</code> block, which applies to every command run inside it.</li>
  </ol>
{% call ui.code_panel('scope_timeout.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, ScopeConfig, TimeoutExpired, scoped, sh

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="timeouts")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)
command = python("-c", "import time; print('started', flush=True); time.sleep(30)")

with scoped(ScopeConfig(timeout=1.0)):
    try:
        command.run_sync()
    except TimeoutExpired as exc:
        assert exc.timeout == 1.0
        # A slow start can expire before the child prints anything.
        assert exc.output in {"", "started\n"}
    else:
        raise AssertionError("the scope timeout should have expired")
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  <p>
    Timeouts must be finite and non-negative; <code>None</code> means no
    limit. Zero or less is treated as a deadline already passed. A pipeline
    enforces one deadline for the whole run and reports its expiry once per
    stage. Every expiry also writes a structured <code>WARNING</code> to the
    <code>cuprum.timeout</code> logger, whether or not a hook is observing.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="descendants">
  <h2 id="descendants">Reclaim the descendants a child leaves behind</h2>
  <p>
    A timeout terminates the process Cuprum spawned, but only that process.
    When the child has spawned descendants of its own, they are not
    signalled, and one that holds an inherited pipe open keeps the run
    waiting out its end-of-file window before <code>TimeoutExpired</code> is
    raised, while the work the deadline was meant to stop carries on.
    {{ api.ref('ExecutionContext') }}<code>.process_group</code> takes a
    {{ api.ref('ProcessGroupPolicy') }}. The default, <code>INHERIT</code>,
    tears down the direct child only; <code>OWN_GROUP</code> makes the child
    lead a new session and process group, so teardown signals the whole
    group and every descendant still in it:
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('process_group.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys

from cuprum import ExecutionContext, ProcessGroupPolicy, Program, ProgramCatalogue, sh

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="process-group")
command = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)(
    "-c", "import os, sys; print(os.getpgrp() == os.getpid())"
)
owned = ExecutionContext(process_group=ProcessGroupPolicy.OWN_GROUP)
assert owned.process_group is ProcessGroupPolicy.OWN_GROUP
assert command.run_sync(context=owned).stdout == "True\n"
assert ExecutionContext().process_group is ProcessGroupPolicy.INHERIT
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  <p>
    The last assertion is the compatibility promise: a context that says
    nothing keeps the child in the caller's group, so no existing run is
    affected. Under <code>OWN_GROUP</code> teardown signals exactly the group
    the spawn created, never the caller's, with the same two-phase grace:
    <code>SIGTERM</code>, <code>cancel_grace</code>, then
    <code>SIGKILL</code>. Settlement is then decided by the group rather than
    the direct child, so a descendant the child left behind is still reached
    after the child has exited.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two trade-offs are worth knowing before opting in. Session leadership
    detaches the child from the controlling terminal: a terminal
    <code>SIGINT</code> or <code>SIGHUP</code> stops at the run rather than
    reaching the child, and a child that opens <code>/dev/tty</code> fails for
    want of one. And the guarantee is bounded by the kernel: a descendant
    that calls <code>setsid()</code> or <code>setpgid()</code> leaves the
    group and is out of reach, as are ancestors and siblings; containing a
    process that actively escapes needs a supervisor, a cgroup, or a
    container. <code>OWN_GROUP</code> raises <code>ValueError</code> at spawn
    on Windows, which has no POSIX process groups and whose Job Objects
    cannot be assigned atomically with process creation; <code>INHERIT</code>
    works everywhere. A teardown outcome Cuprum could not resolve is
    reported as a <code>group_teardown</code> event and a
    <code>WARNING</code>, so a teardown that left a process alive is never
    silent.
  </p>
</section>
{% endblock %}
